<%= render layout: "guides/frame", locals: { guide: @guide } do %>
  <p>
    Most of the time nobody asks to see your DSCSA records. When someone does, the clock is short:
    <strong>two business days</strong>. The pharmacies that handle it calmly are the ones that have practiced once.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>When the two-day clock applies</h2>
    <p>
      Under FD&amp;C Act section 582(d), when FDA or another appropriate federal or state official asks, in a
      <strong>recall</strong> or while <strong>investigating a suspect or illegitimate product</strong>, a
      dispenser must provide the records it received from the previous owner within two business days of the
      request, or another reasonable time FDA sets for the circumstances.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What you&rsquo;ll be asked for</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>transaction information</strong> and <strong>transaction statement</strong> you received when
      you bought the product: what it was, how much, the lot (where provided), who sold it to you, and when.
      You must keep these for at least six years after the transaction, so a request can reach back that far.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>If your wholesaler keeps them for you</h2>
    <p>
      DSCSA allows a written agreement under which a third party, such as an authorized wholesale distributor,
      keeps your transaction records on your behalf. Two conditions come with it: you keep a copy of the written
      agreement, and you remain responsible for producing the records on time. Know how to log in to the portal,
      who has access when you&rsquo;re away, and how far back it goes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A 30-minute drill</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick one product and lot you received a few months ago.</li>
      <li>Start a timer and pull its transaction information and statement, from your files or your
        wholesaler&rsquo;s portal.</li>
      <li>Save them as one PDF named with the product, lot and date.</li>
      <li>Note how long it took, what slowed you down, and who else could do it if you were out.</li>
      <li>Repeat with an older purchase, from a different supplier, to test the reach-back.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Write down who receives an official request, who pulls the records, and who sends them. That half page is
      the procedure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The rest of what applies today</h2>
    <%= render "guides/applies_today" %>
  </section>
<% end %>
